course: dictionary
lesson: 13725
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning you need. Ask yourself: which meaning fits here? «Reduce your sugar intake» is about what you eat and drink, not about a school.
explain: «This year's intake is the biggest we have ever had.» Here intake means the group of new pupils who start at the same time. The words «this year» and «biggest» are your clues.
explain: «She took a sharp intake of breath when the door creaked.» Intake of breath means a quick breath in. Test your meaning in the sentence: «She took a sharp, quick breath in when the door creaked.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Intake means the amount of something you take into your body, like food, water or air.
fact: An intake can also be the group of new people who join a school, college, job or the army at one time.
fact: An intake can be a place or opening where water, air or fuel goes into a pipe or machine.
fact: «Daily intake» means how much of something you have each day.
fact: «Intake of breath» means a quick breath in, often from surprise or shock.
exercise: «Reduce your sugar intake.» What does intake mean here? | *The amount of sugar you take into your body | A quick breath in | The new pupils who start school | The opening where water goes into a pipe
exercise: «This year's intake is the biggest we have ever had.» What is the intake? | The opening where air goes into a machine | The amount of food people eat | *The group of new pupils who start together
exercise: «She gave a sharp intake of breath when the door creaked.» What is an intake of breath? | A sip of cold water | A loud shout for help | *A quick breath in, often from surprise | A long, deep sleep
test: «The water pipe's intake was blocked, so no water could flow in.» What does intake mean here? | The daily amount of water people drink | The new pipes bought this year | *The opening where water goes into the pipe | A loud gurgling noise
test: «Eat a healthy daily intake of fruit and vegetables.» What does daily intake mean? | The day the fruit arrives at the shop | The new class starting in September | *How much you eat each day | A sudden breath in
exam: «The school's intake was 120 new pupils this September.» What does intake mean in this sentence? | A quick breath in | The amount of food eaten each day | *A group of new people who join together | An opening for water or air
